{
  "topicId": "percentages",
  "levels": [
    {
      "id": "percentages-level-0",
      "topicId": "percentages",
      "order": 0,
      "title": "What Does Percentage Mean?",
      "description": "Build the mental model before any formula: a percentage is a part out of one hundred.",
      "type": "concept",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "estimatedMinutes": 6,
      "prerequisiteLevelIds": [],
      "conceptIds": [
        "pct-meaning",
        "pct-conversion"
      ],
      "xpReward": 20,
      "lesson": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "title": "Per cent means \"per hundred\"",
            "body": "The word percent comes from per centum — out of a hundred. When you write 37%, you are writing the fraction 37/100. Nothing more.\n\nThat single idea is the whole topic. Everything else is what happens when the whole is not conveniently 100."
          },
          {
            "type": "formula",
            "title": "The one relationship",
            "body": "Percentage = (Part ÷ Whole) × 100",
            "caption": "Read it as: what fraction is the part of the whole, expressed per hundred."
          },
          {
            "type": "example",
            "title": "Reading it in both directions",
            "body": "45 marks out of 60\n= 45/60\n= 0.75\n= 75%\n\nAnd backwards: 75% of 60\n= (75/100) × 60\n= 45"
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "info",
            "body": "A percentage on its own means nothing — it is always a percentage OF something. Before you calculate, ask: percent of what? Most errors in this topic are answers taken as a percentage of the wrong whole."
          },
          {
            "type": "check",
            "questionId": "percentages-q-001"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "title": "Percentages, fractions and decimals are one idea",
            "body": "These are three notations for the same number. Fluent students switch between them without thinking, because the easiest notation depends on the question.\n\n1/4 = 0.25 = 25%\n1/8 = 0.125 = 12.5%\n1/3 = 0.333… = 33⅓%"
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "success",
            "body": "Learning the common fraction equivalents (1/8 through 1/12) is the highest-value memorisation in arithmetic. 37.5% of 800 is slow; 3/8 of 800 is instant."
          },
          {
            "type": "check",
            "questionId": "percentages-q-002"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "title": "Where this shows up",
            "body": "Profit and loss, discounts, simple and compound interest, data interpretation, population growth and exam marks are all percentage questions wearing different clothes. Getting fluent here is the cheapest way to get better at half the syllabus."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
